{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2342 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "cd4398b2a053ed330502ccb3f3eada7bba51e444b5b6c28c6350eadb68fc1ebc" :edb/db-before-t 2341 :edb/db-after-t 2342 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535654 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:31:48.969Z" 13194139535654 true] [17592186359369 1100 "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139535654 false] [17592186359369 1100 "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nAssume $x>0$." 13194139535654 true] [17592186359369 1145 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535654 false] [17592186359369 1145 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDomain clarification: The problem assumes $x>0$, so $\\sqrt{x^2}=x$ in the source derivation above. Without that assumption, $f(x,x^3)=|x|\\sqrt{1+x^4}$ and\n$$\\frac{df}{dx}=\\operatorname{sgn}(x)\\frac{1+3x^4}{\\sqrt{1+x^4}}\\quad(x\\ne0).$$\nFor $x<0$ the derivative is the negative of the displayed answer. At $x=0$, the one-sided difference quotients tend to $-1$ and $1$, so the derivative does not exist. The original unqualified prompt and worked solution remain preserved in the source capture and database history." 13194139535654 true]] :edb/tempids {}}
